1
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:02,710
This box has never been opened.

2
00:00:05,610 --> 00:00:07,877
The plastic is tight. The seal is intact.

3
00:00:08,777 --> 00:00:12,930
Inside is a genuine hardware wallet. The real chip. The real software.

4
00:00:13,830 --> 00:00:16,805
On 9 October, 335 wallets were emptied.

5
00:00:16,845 --> 00:00:21,330
Ledger tied the confirmed losses to devices like this one.

6
00:00:23,830 --> 00:00:27,399
About $102 million, by one on-chain count.

7
00:00:28,299 --> 00:00:31,278
Nobody broke the chip. Nobody cracked the code.

8
00:00:32,179 --> 00:00:33,408
Someone just read the screen.

9
00:00:39,657 --> 00:00:43,027
I'm Thomas Archer, the AI narrator of Siphon Files.

10
00:00:43,527 --> 00:00:45,945
Every claim in this video comes with a source.

11
00:00:46,545 --> 00:00:48,402
Ledger hasn't confirmed the totals.

12
00:00:48,442 --> 00:00:50,068
So we'll tell you who counted.

13
00:00:53,168 --> 00:00:57,488
On 29 September, one Bitcoin address received 80 coins.

14
00:00:58,188 --> 00:01:00,977
At the time, that was roughly $5 million.

15
00:01:01,678 --> 00:01:04,219
The coins went onto a Ledger. A hardware wallet.

16
00:01:04,259 --> 00:01:07,431
A small device that keeps your keys away from the internet.

17
00:01:08,031 --> 00:01:09,842
It's the advice everyone gets.

18
00:01:09,882 --> 00:01:13,358
Don't leave your coins on an exchange. Buy a cold wallet.

19
00:01:13,398 --> 00:01:16,113
Write the 24 words on paper. Keep them safe.

20
00:01:16,713 --> 00:01:18,642
The device came from CryptoBilis.

21
00:01:18,682 --> 00:01:20,550
Not a stranger on a marketplace.

22
00:01:20,590 --> 00:01:25,482
An authorised Ledger reseller, listed for Malaysia, Indonesia and the Philippines.

23
00:01:26,282 --> 00:01:28,907
But ten days later, the 80 coins were gone.

24
00:01:29,707 --> 00:01:31,971
They left in a single Bitcoin block.

25
00:01:32,011 --> 00:01:34,892
Together with the coins of 110 other wallets.

26
00:01:35,493 --> 00:01:39,702
No phishing link. No fake support call. No malware on a laptop.

27
00:01:40,602 --> 00:01:43,319
So how do you rob a wallet that never goes online?

28
00:01:43,919 --> 00:01:46,289
To answer that, we have to open the box.

29
00:01:50,289 --> 00:01:51,765
A Ledger has two brains.

30
00:01:52,365 --> 00:01:53,755
The first is a secure chip.

31
00:01:53,795 --> 00:01:56,814
It holds your keys, and it's built never to let them out.

32
00:01:57,414 --> 00:01:59,301
The second is an ordinary chip.

33
00:01:59,341 --> 00:02:02,511
It runs the buttons, the Bluetooth. And the screen.

34
00:02:03,111 --> 00:02:05,783
In 2020, Ledger wrote down a rule for the Nano X.

35
00:02:05,823 --> 00:02:10,195
That second chip could be fully compromised, and the funds would still be safe.

36
00:02:12,895 --> 00:02:16,473
Because the secret never leaves the secure chip. Except once.

37
00:02:17,172 --> 00:02:21,588
When you set the device up, it shows you your recovery phrase. 24 words.

38
00:02:21,628 --> 00:02:24,166
On the screen. So you can copy them down.

39
00:02:24,766 --> 00:02:27,741
For a few minutes, your most valuable secret is on display.

40
00:02:28,540 --> 00:02:29,895
Now look behind that screen.

41
00:02:30,495 --> 00:02:33,972
Researchers who opened tampered units found an extra board,

42
00:02:34,012 --> 00:02:38,681
tucked where a strip of padding should be. A microcontroller. A cellular modem.

43
00:02:38,721 --> 00:02:40,152
An antenna. And an eSIM.

44
00:02:42,452 --> 00:02:46,127
To make room, the original battery had been swapped for a smaller one.

45
00:02:46,728 --> 00:02:50,977
Ledger's own security team, the Donjon, described what the implant does.

46
00:02:51,477 --> 00:02:53,685
It reads the data going to the screen.

47
00:02:53,725 --> 00:02:56,583
When it sees the recovery words, it decodes them.

48
00:02:56,623 --> 00:02:59,699
Then it sends them out over LTE. The mobile network.

49
00:03:00,398 --> 00:03:02,010
It doesn't touch the secure chip.

50
00:03:02,050 --> 00:03:04,002
It doesn't change a single transaction.

51
00:03:04,603 --> 00:03:07,071
And it doesn't need your laptop or your phone.

52
00:03:07,111 --> 00:03:08,747
It brought its own connection.

53
00:03:09,446 --> 00:03:12,782
No virus to catch. No link to click. Just a box.

54
00:03:13,682 --> 00:03:16,711
Ledger's genuineness check is built to test the secure chip.

55
00:03:16,751 --> 00:03:19,565
And in these devices, the secure chip was the real one.

56
00:03:23,566 --> 00:03:24,982
But this board wasn't new.

57
00:03:25,582 --> 00:03:29,938
According to Tibane Labs, a research group that documented the implant,

58
00:03:29,978 --> 00:03:35,117
the first public warning about a tampered Nano X appeared on Reddit in August 2025.

59
00:03:35,717 --> 00:03:39,292
This year, the hardware hacker Joe Grand took one apart on stage.

60
00:03:39,332 --> 00:03:43,170
He showed the smaller battery, and the board that shouldn't be there.

61
00:03:43,771 --> 00:03:47,675
On 16 September, Mark Karpelès posted photos of a newer version.

62
00:03:47,715 --> 00:03:49,810
He called it "quite an evolution".

63
00:03:50,410 --> 00:03:55,192
Those research units came from other channels. Online marketplaces in Japan.

64
00:03:55,232 --> 00:03:56,564
Not from CryptoBilis.

65
00:03:57,164 --> 00:04:01,557
So the implant was public. What nobody had yet shown was a wave of real losses.

66
00:04:02,357 --> 00:04:03,611
That was about to change.

67
00:04:04,411 --> 00:04:07,207
Back in March, CryptoBilis had changed hands.

68
00:04:07,807 --> 00:04:10,894
Its founders later said the company was sold to a new team,

69
00:04:10,934 --> 00:04:14,656
and that they handed over all operations. They haven't named the buyer.

70
00:04:14,696 --> 00:04:16,074
They deny any involvement.

71
00:04:16,873 --> 00:04:20,192
Who owned the shop by October? Nobody has said publicly.

72
00:04:24,192 --> 00:04:25,882
So the thief started preparing.

73
00:04:25,922 --> 00:04:27,986
And the blockchain kept the receipts.

74
00:04:28,586 --> 00:04:33,605
On 24 September, a withdrawal from the exchange Bybit landed in a wallet on the

75
00:04:33,645 --> 00:04:34,478
Tron network.

76
00:04:35,078 --> 00:04:37,221
That money later paid the attack's fees.

77
00:04:37,821 --> 00:04:43,679
From the 25th, the thief ran tests. 21 small loops on Tron. 20 on Ethereum.

78
00:04:45,579 --> 00:04:47,119
Remember one thing from this.

79
00:04:47,159 --> 00:04:50,429
A single Tron address, acting as the thief's control wallet.

80
00:04:53,129 --> 00:04:55,835
Meanwhile, the victims kept loading their wallets.

81
00:04:55,875 --> 00:05:00,393
Bitquery found that about 6 in 10 drained wallets were first funded within the last

82
00:05:00,433 --> 00:05:01,033
90 days.

83
00:05:01,472 --> 00:05:04,336
Someone had the keys in advance. And waited.

84
00:05:11,336 --> 00:05:18,465
At 04:59 UTC, 30 Tron wallets got a new key added. Each paid a 100 TRX fee.

85
00:05:19,265 --> 00:05:22,069
The thief didn't steal yet. The thief changed the locks.

86
00:05:25,269 --> 00:05:31,006
At 05:07. 25 Tron wallets sign the same approval, within 3 seconds.

87
00:05:31,607 --> 00:05:34,576
$29 million leaves in 6 seconds.

88
00:05:35,276 --> 00:05:38,817
Then BNB Chain. Polygon. Ethereum. Solana.

89
00:05:39,417 --> 00:05:45,857
At 05:54, one Bitcoin block. 111 wallets, emptied together. The 80 coins were in it.

90
00:05:46,557 --> 00:05:49,500
Another wallet, first funded on 15 September,

91
00:05:49,540 --> 00:05:52,854
lost $7 million in Tether in a single transaction.

92
00:05:53,453 --> 00:05:57,284
One Tron wallet had taken in $33 million since October began.

93
00:05:57,324 --> 00:05:58,974
It looked like a business.

94
00:05:59,575 --> 00:06:05,738
By Bitquery's count, the full tally reached $102 million, from 335 wallets,

95
00:06:05,778 --> 00:06:06,936
on 6 networks.

96
00:06:07,935 --> 00:06:10,474
Most of it was gone before anyone raised the alarm.

97
00:06:13,575 --> 00:06:15,265
But stablecoins can be frozen.

98
00:06:15,305 --> 00:06:17,207
And by noon, Tether was freezing.

99
00:06:17,807 --> 00:06:20,238
The first freeze came at 12:09 UTC.

100
00:06:20,278 --> 00:06:23,456
7 hours after the first wallets were drained.

101
00:06:25,556 --> 00:06:27,487
About $10 million stayed locked.

102
00:06:29,387 --> 00:06:31,858
The thief had planned for that, too.

103
00:06:31,898 --> 00:06:37,128
$23.7 million in Tether was swapped into USDD, a token Tether can't freeze.

104
00:06:37,828 --> 00:06:42,110
The rest moved through THORChain, Chainflip and other swap services.

105
00:06:42,150 --> 00:06:44,248
Into Tornado Cash. Through Zcash.

106
00:06:45,048 --> 00:06:49,477
Back on the 9th itself, Mark Karpelès opened a Nano X sent to him from Malaysia.

107
00:06:49,517 --> 00:06:51,528
It arrived still in its shrink-wrap.

108
00:06:52,128 --> 00:06:55,179
Behind the screen, where the padding should be, he found the board.

109
00:06:56,080 --> 00:07:00,012
On 10 October, Ledger confirmed an implant in one victim's device.

110
00:07:00,052 --> 00:07:02,420
On the 13th, it described the LTE board.

111
00:07:02,460 --> 00:07:06,733
It said every confirmed loss involved devices sold through CryptoBilis.

112
00:07:07,333 --> 00:07:10,772
Two days later, it blamed "a sophisticated criminal group",

113
00:07:10,812 --> 00:07:14,605
and tightened the rules for its resellers. No devices on display.

114
00:07:14,645 --> 00:07:17,358
Handed over at purchase. No resale of returns.

115
00:07:17,958 --> 00:07:22,766
Then Singapore police reported at least 2 cases since 10 September.

116
00:07:22,806 --> 00:07:26,496
About S$3.5 million. Devices bought in local shops.

117
00:07:27,096 --> 00:07:31,303
In Europe, the electronics chain MediaMarkt paused its Ledger sales.

118
00:07:32,103 --> 00:07:34,885
So it wasn't just one reseller, in one country.

119
00:07:38,884 --> 00:07:45,565
Then, on 14 November, at 19:43 UTC, 4 Tron wallets emptied.

120
00:07:46,165 --> 00:07:50,238
10 minutes later, 9 Bitcoin wallets, in one block. Then more.

121
00:07:50,838 --> 00:07:54,088
This time, the devices didn't come from Southeast Asia.

122
00:07:54,128 --> 00:07:58,494
A Ledger Flex and a Ledger Stax bought in Dubai. A Flex bought in Berlin.

123
00:07:59,094 --> 00:08:02,469
$1.68 million, from 16 wallets.

124
00:08:04,368 --> 00:08:08,520
And in the same block as the drain, one Tron address paid the network fees.

125
00:08:09,320 --> 00:08:12,318
According to Bitquery, it was the same control address.

126
00:08:13,218 --> 00:08:16,824
On 19 November, Ledger wrote that it is the same incident.

127
00:08:16,864 --> 00:08:20,825
Tampered devices outside Southeast Asia. And more may be found.

128
00:08:21,525 --> 00:08:26,233
Ledger now offers worried owners an X-ray inspection at its factory in Vierzon,

129
00:08:26,273 --> 00:08:26,873
France.

130
00:08:30,693 --> 00:08:33,649
If you own a hardware wallet bought from a shop or a reseller,

131
00:08:33,689 --> 00:08:35,138
here's what the police advise.

132
00:08:35,738 --> 00:08:37,978
If you suspect the device, stop using it.

133
00:08:38,018 --> 00:08:40,203
Don't type your recovery phrase into it.

134
00:08:40,243 --> 00:08:42,857
Contact the maker through its official support.

135
00:08:43,558 --> 00:08:47,105
If you think the words have leaked, move what's left to a new wallet,

136
00:08:47,145 --> 00:08:50,108
with a new recovery phrase, set up on a device you trust.

137
00:08:50,808 --> 00:08:52,014
And never share those words.

138
00:08:52,054 --> 00:08:54,594
Not with someone who says they're from the wallet company.

139
00:08:54,634 --> 00:08:56,636
Not with someone who says they're the police.

140
00:09:00,836 --> 00:09:04,668
As we make this video, we found no public arrest and no charges.

141
00:09:05,369 --> 00:09:08,533
We don't know who fitted the boards, or where along the chain.

142
00:09:08,573 --> 00:09:10,485
We don't know who bought CryptoBilis.

143
00:09:11,185 --> 00:09:14,432
Ledger hasn't confirmed how many devices were tampered with,

144
00:09:14,472 --> 00:09:16,116
or how much was lost in total.

145
00:09:16,816 --> 00:09:18,818
And we don't know if it's over.

146
00:09:18,858 --> 00:09:21,756
Ledger itself says more devices may surface.

147
00:09:22,556 --> 00:09:25,026
Here's what we do know. The chip was never broken.

148
00:09:27,726 --> 00:09:31,638
Somewhere, there's a box like this one. Plastic tight. Seal intact.

149
00:09:32,337 --> 00:09:35,635
A seal proves the box hasn't been opened since someone closed it.

150
00:09:35,675 --> 00:09:37,780
It doesn't tell you who that someone was.

151
00:09:38,580 --> 00:09:42,356
And the thief's money trail starts with a withdrawal from an exchange.

152
00:09:42,396 --> 00:09:43,650
Exchanges keep records.

153
00:09:44,250 --> 00:09:48,166
Whether those records lead to a name, no one has said. So far.

154
00:09:49,167 --> 00:09:51,738
Before you trust a seal, ask who sealed it.

155
00:09:52,737 --> 00:09:55,148
I'm Thomas Archer. This was Siphon Files.

